[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
```js
// MapLibre GL JS (v6) – no default export
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// or, import only what you need
import { Map, NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
```

When a layer’s `icon-image` refers to a name that isn’t present in the style’s sprite, MapLibre GL JS v6 gives you a hook to supply the image yourself.  
The old `styleimagemissing` handler that called `map.addImage()` directly no longer resolves the request – it’s now just a notification.  
Instead you register a **missing-style-image resolver** with `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()`.  
The resolver receives the missing image id, you create or fetch the image, add it to the map, and the pending style-image request is automatically satisfied.

### Step-by-step

1. **Create the map (ESM import)**  

   ```js
   import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
   import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

   const map = new maplibregl.Map({
     container: 'map',
     style: 'https://example.com/your-style.json',
     center: [0, 0],
     zoom: 2,
   });
   ```

2. **Register the resolver** – do this **once**, before any layers that might need missing icons are rendered.

   ```js
   // The resolver can be async; MapLibre will wait for the promise to settle.
   map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (missingId) => {
     // 1️⃣  Decide how you obtain the image.
     //    • Generate it on the fly (e.g., using Canvas or SVG)
     //    • Fetch a remote PNG/SVG
     //    • Look it up in an in-memory cache
     const image = await getIconForId(missingId);   // ← your own function

     // 2️⃣  Add the image to the map.
     //    The second argument can be an HTMLImageElement, HTMLCanvasElement,
     //    ImageBitmap, or an object { width, height, data }.
     map.addImage(missingId, image);
     // No need to return anything – MapLibre ignores the return value.
   });
   ```

3. **Implement `getIconForId`** (example using a remote PNG)

   ```js
   async function getIconForId(id) {
     // Example: icons are stored under /icons/<id>.png
     const response = await fetch(`/icons/${id}.png`);
     if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Icon ${id} not found`);
     const blob = await response.blob();
     // Create an ImageBitmap (fast for WebGL textures)
     return await createImageBitmap(blob);
   }
   ```

   *Or* generate a canvas-based icon:

   ```js
   async function getIconForId(id) {
     const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
     canvas.width = 32;
     canvas.height = 32;
     const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

     // simple example: draw a colored circle with the id as text
     ctx.fillStyle = '#ff6600';
     ctx.beginPath();
     ctx.arc(16, 16, 14, 0, Math.PI * 2);
     ctx.fill();

     ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
     ctx.font = '12px sans-serif';
     ctx.textAlign = 'center';
     ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
     ctx.fillText(id, 16, 16);

     // Return the canvas itself – MapLibre will copy it internally.
     return canvas;
   }
   ```

4. **Use the icon name in your style**  

   ```json
   {
     "id": "my-point-layer",
     "type": "symbol",
     "source": "my-source",
     "layout": {
       "icon-image": "dynamic-icon-name",   // <-- not in the sprite
       "icon-size": 1
     }
   }
   ```

   When the style is applied, MapLibre will fire the resolver with `"dynamic-icon-name"`. Once you add the image, the layer renders automatically.

### Things to keep in mind

| Situation | What you need to do |
|-----------|---------------------|
| **CDN-loaded ESM build** (`<script type="module">`) | No extra worker setup required. Just register the resolver as shown. |
| **Bundler (Vite, webpack, esbuild, etc.)** | Ensure the worker URL is set **once** before creating the map: <br>`import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';`<br>`import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';`<br>`setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);` |
| **Multiple missing icons** | The resolver is called for each missing id. Cache the generated/fetched images if you expect repeats, to avoid redundant work. |
| **Performance** | Prefer `createImageBitmap` (or `HTMLImageElement`/`HTMLCanvasElement`) over raw pixel data; the bitmap is already GPU-ready. |
| **Error handling** | If the resolver throws or returns a rejected promise, the style image stays missing and the layer will render without an icon. You can fall back to a default image inside the resolver. |

### Full minimal example (Vite-style project)

```js
// src/main.ts
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// Tell MapLibre where the worker lives (required for bundlers)
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: '/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

// Register the resolver
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Example: fetch PNG from /icons/<id>.png
  const resp = await fetch(`/icons/${id}.png`);
  if (!resp.ok) {
    console.warn(`Icon ${id} not found – using fallback`);
    return; // leave it missing; you could add a fallback image here
  }
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(await resp.blob());
  map.addImage(id, bitmap);
});
```

Now any `icon-image` value that isn’t present in the sprite will be supplied at runtime, and the map will display the newly added icons without a page reload.

Below is a minimal v6-compatible example that shows how to read the public camera state (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) and, when you need the actual projection-view matrix, how to obtain it from a **custom layer** – the only place the matrix is still exposed after the `map.transform` removal.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre camera state + projection matrix</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Import the ESM build (v6 is ESM-only)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // lng, lat
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17,
    // No need for setWorkerUrl() when loading from a CDN
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Public camera getters (always available)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  function logPublicCameraState() {
    const center = map.getCenter();          // → LngLat object
    const zoom   = map.getZoom();            // → number
    const bearing = map.getBearing();        // → number (degrees)
    const pitch   = map.getPitch();          // → number (degrees)

    console.log('center:', center);
    console.log('zoom:',   zoom);
    console.log('bearing:', bearing);
    console.log('pitch:',   pitch);
  }

  // Log once the map is ready
  map.on('load', () => {
    logPublicCameraState();
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Getting the projection-view matrix
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // The matrix is **not** exposed on the Map object any more.
  // The only public entry point is inside a custom layer’s
  // `render` method, where MapLibre passes a `CustomRenderMethodInput`
  // that contains `getProjectionData()`. That method returns an
  // object with the 4×4 matrix (`projectionMatrix`) and the
  // viewport size.
  //
  // Below we add a no-op custom layer just to read the matrix.
  // You can replace the body with whatever math you need.
  const matrixReaderLayer = {
    id: 'matrix-reader',
    type: 'custom',
    // The layer must be added *after* the style is loaded.
    onAdd: (map, gl) => {
      // nothing to initialise
    },
    render: (gl, matrix, projectionData) => {
      // `projectionData` is an instance of `ProjectionData`
      // (see the API docs). It contains:
      //   - projectionMatrix: Float32Array[16]
      //   - viewMatrix:       Float32Array[16] (if you need it)
      //   - width, height
      const { projectionMatrix, viewMatrix, width, height } = projectionData;

      console.log('Projection matrix (4×4):', projectionMatrix);
      console.log('View matrix (4×4):', viewMatrix);
      console.log('Viewport size:', width, '×', height);

      // Example: compute a world-space point from a screen point
      // (you would normally do this in your own math code)
      // const screenX = 100, screenY = 150;
      // const ndcX = (2 * screenX) / width - 1;
      // const ndcY = 1 - (2 * screenY) / height;
      // const clip = [ndcX, ndcY, 0, 1];
      // // Invert the combined matrix to go back to world space
      // const inv = mat4.invert([], mat4.multiply([], projectionMatrix, viewMatrix));
      // const world = vec4.transformMat4([], clip, inv);
      // console.log('World coordinates for screen point:', world);
    }
  };

  // Add the custom layer after the style is loaded
  map.on('styledata', () => {
    if (!map.getLayer('matrix-reader')) {
      map.addLayer(matrixReaderLayer);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the snippet does

| Step | What you get |
|------|--------------|
| **Public getters** (`map.getCenter()`, `map.getZoom()`, `map.getBearing()`, `map.getPitch()`) | Simple, stable API for the camera state. |
| **Projection matrix** | Inside `render(gl, matrix, projectionData)` of a custom layer you receive `projectionData.projectionMatrix` (a `Float32Array` of length 16). This is the exact matrix that the renderer uses to transform world coordinates to clip space. |
| **Why we can’t call `map.transform`** | `map.transform` was removed in v6; the matrix is no longer a public property of `Map`. The custom-layer entry point is the officially supported way to reach it. |

#### Quick notes

* If you only need the matrix for occasional calculations, you can keep the custom layer “no-op” (as shown) and read the matrix from the console or store it in a variable for later use.
* The `render` callback runs on every frame while the map is animating (e.g., during zoom/pan). If you only need the matrix once, you can detach the layer after the first call.
* The example uses the CDN ESM build (`dist/maplibre-gl.mjs`). When using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.) you must import from `'maplibre-gl'` and call `setWorkerUrl()` once, but the camera-state API stays the same.

That’s all you need to retrieve the full camera state—including the projection matrix—in a MapLibre GL JS v6 application. Happy coding!

```ts
import type {
  Map,
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

// Helper type-guard – tells TypeScript that the event is a source-data event
function isSourceDataEvent(
  e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent,
): e is MapSourceDataEvent {
  return 'sourceDataType' in e;
}

// The actual handler you can pass to `map.on('data', …)`
function onMapData(e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent): void {
  if (isSourceDataEvent(e)) {
    // ✅ This branch is only for source-data events
    // `e.sourceDataType` is now strongly typed:
    //   'metadata' | 'source' | 'tile' | 'features' | 'all'
    switch (e.sourceDataType) {
      case 'metadata':
        console.log('Metadata loaded for source', e.sourceId);
        break;
      case 'source':
        console.log('Source data ready', e.sourceId);
        break;
      case 'tile':
        console.log('Tile data loaded', e.sourceId, e.tile?.key);
        break;
      case 'features':
        console.log('Features loaded for source', e.sourceId);
        break;
      case 'all':
        console.log('All source data loaded for', e.sourceId);
        break;
    }
  } else {
    // ✅ This branch handles style-data events
    // No `sourceDataType` here – only style-related info
    console.log('Style data event:', e);
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Usage – attach the handler to a MapLibre map instance
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
});

map.on('data', onMapData);
```

Below is a minimal, **Vite + TypeScript** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** (npm install).  
It shows the required imports, the CSS import, the one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call that Vite needs, and a simple map-initialisation.

```ts
// src/main.ts --------------------------------------------------------------
import { Map, NavigationControl, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Vite-specific way to get the worker URL.
// The `?worker&url` query tells Vite to treat the file as a web-worker
// and return its final URL after bundling.
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// Tell MapLibre where the worker lives – must be called **once**
// before any Map instance is created.
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Create the map
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div> in index.html
  style:    'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
  center:   [0, 0],               // [lng, lat]
  zoom:     2,
  // optional: you can also set pitch, bearing, etc.
});

// Add a default UI control (optional)
map.addControl(new NavigationControl());

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example of handling a missing style image with the v6 API
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // e.g. generate a simple SVG data-URL on-the-fly
  const svg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="64" height="64">
                 <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="30" fill="orange"/>
               </svg>`;
  const img = new Image();
  img.src = `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,${encodeURIComponent(svg)}`;
  await img.decode();               // wait until the image is ready
  map.addImage(id, img);
});
```

```html
<!-- public/index.html ---------------------------------------------------- -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Vite + MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <style>
    /* make the map fill the viewport */
    html, body, #map { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <script type="module" src="/src/main.ts"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why each piece is needed (v6-specific points)

| What changed in v6? | How the code reflects it |
|---------------------|--------------------------|
| **No default export** – you must use named/namespace imports. | `import { Map, NavigationControl, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';` |
| **CommonJS `require()` is no longer supported** – Vite already runs ESM, so the above import works. |
| **Worker URL must be set manually in bundlers** – Vite cannot resolve `import.meta.url` for the worker file. | `import workerUrl … ?worker&url` + `setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);` |
| **`styleimagemissing` is now notify-only** – you need `setMissingStyleImageResolver`. | The resolver added after map creation. |
| **`map.transform` removed** – use the public camera getters (`getZoom()`, `getCenter()`, …) if you ever need them. (Not shown here because the example doesn’t need them.) |

### Quick checklist for a Vite + TS project

1. **Install**  
   ```bash
   npm i maplibre-gl
   # (or yarn/pnpm)
   ```

2. **Add the CSS** – either via the import shown above or by referencing the file in `index.html`.

3. **Configure Vite** – no extra config is required; the `?worker&url` import works out-of-the-box with Vite ≥ 4. If you’re on an older Vite version, upgrade or use the manual `new URL(..., import.meta.url)` pattern:

   ```ts
   setWorkerUrl(new URL('maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs', import.meta.url).toString());
   ```

4. **Run**  
   ```bash
   npm run dev   # Vite dev server
   ```

That’s all you need to get a MapLibre map rendering in a Vite + TypeScript environment with the v6 API. Happy mapping!

Below is a complete, **v6-only** example that

1. registers the **PMTiles** protocol,
2. adds a vector-tile source that reads from a local `.pmtiles` file, and
3. draws a simple line layer from one of the source’s layers.

You can copy-paste the HTML (or adapt the JavaScript for a bundler) and it will work with **MapLibre GL JS v6** and **@protomaps/pmtiles**.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the two packages

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm i maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

> **Why these two?**  
> *`maplibre-gl`* provides the map engine.  
> *`@protomaps/pmtiles`* implements the `pmtiles://` URL protocol that lets MapLibre read a `.pmtiles` archive as a vector-tile source.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal HTML + JS (CDN-only, no bundler)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles → MapLibre line layer</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- 1️⃣ MapLibre v6 ESM build -->
  <script type="module">
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Imports (v6 uses named/namespace imports – no default export)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js';

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // The URL you pass to the protocol can be any string you like – it
    // just has to be unique for each archive you want to expose.
    const pmtilesUrl = new URL('my-data.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString(); // <-- change this path
    const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
    // `pmtiles.Protocol` implements the fetch-handler expected by MapLibre.
    const protocol = new pm.Protocol();

    // Tell MapLibre to use the handler for the "pmtiles" scheme.
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create the map
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
        layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
      },
      center: [0, 0],         // arbitrary start position
      zoom: 2
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the vector source & line layer
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 4a️⃣ Vector source that reads from the .pmtiles file ----
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom scheme we just registered.
        // The part after "pmtiles://" is the identifier we gave the archive.
        url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'
      });

      // ---- 4b️⃣ Line layer ------------------------------------------------
      // You need to know the name of the layer inside the vector tiles.
      // You can discover it with `map.getStyle().sources['my-pmtiles-source']....`
      // or by inspecting the archive with `pmtiles info my-data.pmtiles`.
      const sourceLayerName = 'roads';   // <-- replace with your actual layer name

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-line-layer',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
        'source-layer': sourceLayerName,
        layout: {
          // optional: smooth line joins / caps
          'line-join': 'round',
          'line-cap': 'round'
        },
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',   // bright orange
          'line-width': 3,
          'line-opacity': 0.9
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

### What you need to change

| Placeholder | What to replace | Why |
|-------------|----------------|-----|
| `my-data.pmtiles` (two occurrences) | Path/URL to **your** `.pmtiles` file (relative to the page or an absolute URL) | The PMTiles library loads the archive from this location. |
| `roads` (the `sourceLayerName`) | The exact name of the vector-tile layer that contains the line geometry you want to draw | You can discover it with `pmtiles info my-data.pmtiles` or by opening the archive in a viewer. |
| `center` / `zoom` | Any initial view you prefer | Optional – the map will automatically pan/zoom to the data once you add a `fitBounds` call if you like. |

---

## 3️⃣ If you’re using a bundler (Vite, webpack, Rollup, …)

The same code works, you just import from the installed packages instead of the CDN:

```js
// src/main.js
import { Map, addProtocol } from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';

// Register protocol (same as above)
const pm = new PMTiles(new URL('my-data.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString());
addProtocol('pmtiles', new pm.Protocol().tile);

// …create the map and add source/layer exactly as shown earlier…
```

**Bundler-only note:** v6 still requires a one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call when the code is bundled (see the migration guide). If you hit a “worker failed to load” error, add:

```js
import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

(For a plain CDN `<script type="module">` you **do not** need this call.)

---

## 4️⃣ Quick sanity-check checklist

- ✅ **v6 import style** – `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` or named imports. No default export.
- ✅ **PMTiles protocol** – `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile)`.
- ✅ **Vector source** – `type: 'vector'`, `url: 'pmtiles://<identifier>'`.
- ✅ **Line layer** – `type: 'line'`, correct `source-layer` name, paint properties.
- ✅ **Worker URL** – only needed for bundlers, not for CDN ESM.

That’s it! Your map will now read vector tiles directly from the `.pmtiles` archive and render the requested line features. Happy mapping!
